            <ArticleTitle>Does decompression diminish the discomfort of severe dysthyroid orbitopathy?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>109-12</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We assessed whether patients perceived that surgical decompression had reduced the discomfort of dysthyroid orbitopathy. Standard pain instruments were administered to 13 consecutive patients of a university-based oculoplastic practice who had undergone two-wall orbital decompression for dysthyroid optic neuropathy. We used visual analog scale (VAS) ratings of pre- and postoperative orbital discomfort. On a 0 to 10 scale, relief of discomfort after decompression was rated as moderate or better (VAS &gt; 9.0) in 13 of 13 patients (mean VAS = 9.22, SD = 0.91) and complete (VAS = 10.0) in 8 of 13. Patients perceived that surgical decompression was associated with a clinically and statistically significant (p &lt; 0.001) reduction of discomfort.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
